{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh asks, 2026-10-09: \"Send this to planner in the workroom, requesting a dated design note to describe the full design in detail; not prioritized for implementation.\"\n\nPlease own or assign a DESIGN-ONLY delivery: a dated, detailed repository note on durable condition subscriptions and outbound webhooks. Suggested path notes/2026-10-09-condition-subscriptions-and-webhooks.md; use the actual authoring date if later. Return the reviewable note/artifact, a concise summary of choices and unresolved questions. Do not prioritize or dispatch runtime implementation. Identify future prerequisites without silently adopting a new protocol.\n\nDesign brief from the discussion:\nArtroom should offer durable condition subscriptions; HTTP webhooks are one delivery channel. A subscription means \"when this condition becomes true for an exact subject/version, record a notification and deliver it to an authorized endpoint.\" Familiar GitHub/Forgejo delivery conventions are useful, while Artroom can expose conditions over recorded work beyond a fixed event menu.\nExamples: notify when a change affecting docs/** becomes ready for review, once per proposal version; notify when an issue remains unassigned for two hours. These are illustrative requirements, not existing APIs.\n\nDataflow: committed events -\u003e subscription evaluator with versioned definition and durable cursor -\u003e durable condition match with subject/version/evidence/notification ID and frozen payload -\u003e delivery outbox -\u003e signed HTTP POST -\u003e external acknowledgment -\u003e recorded attempts/outcomes. Endpoint availability must not delay or change the original room decision. A timeout affects notification delivery, not whether a change merged.\n\nThe full note should specify:\n\n1. Goals/non-goals and terminology: source event, evaluation, condition match, notification, delivery attempt, acknowledgment and external processing. Explain Artroom's recorded publication versus a raw Git-host push; provider signals cannot alone imply a valid Artroom outcome.\n\n2. Subscription schema and authorized lifecycle: create/edit/pause/resume/remove, condition revision/digest, exact scope/dependencies and subject/grouping key, payload-field selection, endpoint/private-secret references, activation cursor, expiration and budgets. Separate nonsecret configuration from private credential custody. Include proposed configuration, payload and delivery-record examples.\n\n3. Bounded declarative conditions using existing Guard/Condition vocabulary where sound: events, transitions, labels/actors/paths/state/counts/relationships, time windows and correlated facts. Define dependency indexing, validation across pinned schema revisions, evaluation budgets and compilation. No unrestricted JS or external network callbacks in matching. A broader authorized event feed with receiver-side evaluation is the escape hatch for complex arbitrary logic.\n\n4. Precise firing semantics: each event, false-to-true transition, once per subject/version, repeated true state, rearming, grouping, subscription edits, explicit historical backfill and expiry. Default state predicates should trigger entering the matching state, not every unrelated event while true. Unknown/incomplete evidence is not false. Define persisted deadline/timer evaluation with no browser dependence.\n\n5. Local and cross-scope truth: exact facts/observed heads/causal cut supporting each match, delayed/out-of-order information, freshness, replay, absence/negative conditions and dependent observation phases. Do not imply an atomic snapshot across independent scopes. Distinguish advisory observation from recorded judgments and state what the external notification asserts.\n\n6. Crash-safe event processing: advance cursor and record resulting notifications atomically, with stable match identity across restart and duplicate source deliveries. Process relevant committed events in sequence. Coalesced head streams can wake an evaluator, but cannot replace durable enumeration: a condition may become true then false between observations. Specify bounded catch-up, retention/history floors, initial activation and subscription-version handling.\n\n7. Delivery/recovery: frozen match/evidence/payload before HTTP; stable notification ID, separate attempt IDs; retry/backoff/budgets, temporary/permanent failures, paused/expired/terminal states, manual redelivery and inspection. Redelivery must not reevaluate newer state. Lost acknowledgment means receiver may have processed it. Receiver deduplicates stable notification identity; 2xx establishes endpoint acceptance, not completed downstream work. No exactly-once external-effect guarantee. Qualify at-least-once/retry language by endpoint availability, budgets and authorization.\n\n8. Fit with existing operation guarantees: Outside.send makes one request per marked attempt, with unknown outcomes; do not silently repeat a mutation. Define an explicit webhook owner/policy permitting duplicate-tolerant NEW attempts of one notification while preserving pre-send durable marks, exact attempt identity and recorded results. Compare reuse of existing operations/outbox with a separate transport; do not add a generic relay by default.\n\n9. Wire/receiver contract: assess CloudEvents source+id/type/subject/time/schema version, plus subscription revision, subject version and provenance. Specify exact payload bytes, signing/authentication, timestamp/delivery binding, replay checks, secret rotation and deduplication. Receiver durably accepts/deduplicates before 2xx and processes asynchronously.\n\n10. Authorized export: integration-management/egress grants, dependency and field permissions, creation versus dispatch-time authorization, revocation and pause effects on queued/in-flight notices, private signing-key custody. Default small useful facts plus authenticated links; full private content export is explicit. Cover HTTPS/destination restrictions/SSRF/redirects, bounded requests/responses, rate limits, endpoint verification and secret-safe logs as the concrete webhook boundary.\n\n11. APIs/person-facing controls: create, test, edit, pause/resume, remove, inspect matches/attempts, explain fired/not-fired conditions, retry a known notification, explicit backfill. Test messages must be distinct from real matches. Keep ordinary controls concise/action-oriented, with detailed provenance on inspection.\n\n12. Resource/latency design: no endpoint wait, browser heartbeat, full-history scan or arbitrary subscriber code on the authoritative commit path. Describe durable trigger placement and wake recovery, quotas, fan-out/fairness/concurrency and ordering. Coordinate with the existing responsiveness/query/notification assessment without creating implementation priority or duplicate tasks.\n\n13. Current foundation/gaps/alternatives/decisions: cite actual inspected HEAD and existing owners. Distinguish available primitives from proposed capabilities and required protocol/definition versions. Discuss future staging (local events/transitions plus signed retryable delivery/inspector, then timed and explicitly defined cross-scope matching) as design options, not a commissioned implementation roadmap.\n\n14. Verification design: current/proposed dataflow diagrams and crash/recovery sequences; meaningful witnesses for transient matches, repeated true state, duplicates, subscription edits/backfill, deadlines without browser, cursor+match crash boundaries, lost HTTP ack/deduplication, ordering, incomplete/stale evidence, authorization revocation, secret rotation, retries/expiry and resource budgets. Prefer existing invariant owners and cheapest relevant boundaries; no runtime changes or repeated whole suites are needed to write the note.\n\nInspected foundations (recheck at actual note HEAD):\n- packages/contract/src/definition.ts:170-238 provides Guard, Condition, conditional effects/sends and member Notify; it is NOT an implemented generic external-webhook API.\n- packages/scope/src/operations.ts:95-140 defines per-attempt send, unknown outcome, late-answer and safe-recovery semantics.\n- Scope logs, retained evidence, timed rules, outbox duties and authenticated head streams are relevant. Head coalescing must not erase transient condition occurrences.\n- No generic webhook implementation was found in the discussion's inspected runtime.\n\nPrimary references checked:\nGitHub conventions https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/using-webhooks/best-practices-for-using-webhooks\nSignature validation https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/using-webhooks/validating-webhook-deliveries\nGitHub delivery behavior https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/using-webhooks (do not attribute proposed automatic retries to GitHub; it does not automatically redeliver every failed hook)\nForgejo https://forgejo.org/docs/latest/user/repository/webhooks/\nCloudEvents https://github.com/cloudevents/spec/blob/main/cloudevents/spec.md\nIf Cloudflare Queues is considered, verify https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/reference/delivery-guarantees/ and https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/reference/how-queues-works/ : duplicates are possible and ordering is not guaranteed; Queues is not required by this brief.\n\nDeliver ONLY the dated detailed design note and its review/report. Mark runtime implementation as NOT PRIORITIZED; do not commission it from this request.","body":{"conditions":"Deliver a dated, detailed, reviewable repository design note on condition subscriptions/webhooks, with schemas, semantics, architecture, authorization, delivery/recovery, APIs, alternatives, diagrams and verification. Return artifact and unresolved choices. No runtime implementation commissioning or prioritization.","design_only":"true","implementation_priority":"not prioritized","target_head":"f6d80b3996df7ad3e7d00feff88d48cf29877ddc","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}